course: chemistry
lesson: 8
title: Separating mixtures
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A mixture is things mixed together but not joined, so you can get them apart again if you pick the right trick.
explain: Each trick uses a difference between the things: size for a sieve or a colander, magnetism for a magnet, weight for settling, and boiling for salt water.
explain: Draining pasta is a separation: the holes of the colander are big enough for water but too small for the pasta.
explain: Dissolved things are harder: salt in water passes through any filter, so you must let the water evaporate to leave the salt behind, or boil it off and catch the steam to get pure water.
explain: Even the colours in black felt-tip ink can be pulled apart on wet kitchen paper.
fact: A colander or a filter lets small things through and keeps bigger things back; a tea bag is a filter too.
fact: A sieve lets fine flour through and keeps lumps; long ago gold miners used pans and sieves to wash gold from river gravel.
fact: Filter paper traps soil grains in muddy water, but filtered water is not safe to drink: germs and dissolved things pass through.
fact: When salty water evaporates, the salt stays behind as crystals; sea salt is made this way in sunny places.
fact: A magnet pulls out iron and steel things but not sand; aluminium cans are not picked up by a magnet.
fact: Things can be sorted by size, shape or colour; factory machines use cameras to sort beans or nuts very fast.
fact: Left to stand, the heavier bits in muddy water settle to the bottom; water works use settling tanks.
fact: Distillation is boiling a liquid and cooling the steam back to liquid; the drops are pure water, and the salt stays behind.
fact: Ships and some dry countries make drinking water from seawater by distillation and other methods.
fact: Chromatography separates the colours in ink because they travel with water at different speeds; it has been used to check which pen wrote a note.
fact: A salad spinner flings the water outward and off the leaves through the holes; hospitals spin blood fast to separate it into layers.
exercise: You pour pasta into a colander. Why does the pasta stay behind? | The holes let water through but are too small for pasta | The hot pasta sticks to the metal of the colander like glue | The water is heavier than pasta | The colander soaks up the water
exercise: What does sieving flour for a cake do? | Lets fine flour through and keeps the lumps | Turns some of the flour into sugar so the cake is sweeter | Makes the flour heavier | Mixes air bubbles into the eggs
exercise: Muddy water goes through a coffee filter. What happens? | The soil is trapped and clearer water passes | The water is made completely safe and clean to drink | The mud dissolves in the paper | Nothing passes through at all
exercise: Salty water is left in a saucer on a sunny windowsill. What is left after a few days? | White salt crystals | A little pool of pure water | Nothing at all, not a speck | A layer of brown sandy mud
exercise: How could you get paper clips out of a pile of sand? | Use a magnet | Use a sieve with big holes | Add water and stir | Blow on the sand
exercise: You pick the raisins out of your cereal by hand. What are you using to separate them? | How they look: their shape and colour | How strongly a magnet pulls on them | Their smell under water | How fast they dissolve
exercise: What happens to muddy water left to stand in a jar? | The mud settles to the bottom | The mud slowly floats up and makes a thick layer at the top | The mud dissolves completely away | All the water slowly turns into mud
exercise: Salty water boils in a pan. Are the drops on the cold lid salty? | No, they are pure water, the salt stays in the pan | Yes, they are just as salty as the water in the pan | Yes, even saltier than the pan | They are pure salt with no water
exercise: A drop of black felt-tip on wet kitchen paper spreads into rings of colour. Why? | The colours travel with the water at different speeds | The kitchen paper has colours hidden in it, and the water lets them out | The water turns the ink into a rainbow | Light shines through the wet paper
exercise: How does a salad spinner dry lettuce? | Spinning flings the water out | It blows warm air over the leaves until all the water has dried | It squeezes the leaves flat and dry | The basket soaks up all the water
exercise: What is distillation? | Boiling a liquid and cooling the steam back to liquid | Pouring a liquid slowly through a sheet of paper to catch the bits | Letting a liquid stand still | Freezing a liquid to make ice
exercise: How could you separate rice from flour? | Use a sieve: flour falls through, rice stays | Use a strong magnet to pull out the rice | Add water and boil | Stir it fast
exercise: Why is filtered pond water still not safe to drink? | Germs and dissolved things can pass through the filter | The filter paper adds a poison to the water | Filtered water is always far too cold to drink | Pond water is always safe to drink as long as it looks clear
exercise: What is the name for separating ink colours on wet paper? | Chromatography | Distillation | Evaporation | Filtration
test: Your friend says: "A magnet will pull all the cans out of the recycling." Is that right? | Only steel cans, aluminium cans are not attracted | Yes, every can is magnetic, whatever metal it is made of | No, magnets only pick up gold | Only if the cans are wet
test: What would happen if you tried to filter salt out of salty water with a coffee filter? | The salt would pass straight through, dissolved | The salt would be caught and stay behind in the filter | The water would stay in the filter | The salt would turn into sand
test: "why are salt pans in hot sunny places, not in rainy old Wales" Best answer? | Sun and warmth evaporate the water, rain keeps adding more | Salt is only ever found in the seas near the hot countries of the world | Rain makes salt explode | Cold seas have no salt
test: A detective cartoon says "every black pen has the same ink, so we can't tell which wrote the note." What's wrong? | Chromatography can show which colours are in each pen's ink | Nothing, because every black ink in the world is exactly the same | Ink can only be tested with fire | Pens can only be told apart by their smell
test: A ship has no fresh water but plenty of seawater. What could they do? | Make fresh water by distilling or another desalting method | Filter the seawater through a clean cloth to catch the salt | Leave it in a bucket to settle | Freeze it and eat the salt
test: "mud in this jar's taking ages to sink, can I speed it up" Which of these would actually help? | Spinning it fast, as hospitals spin blood | Shaking the jar very hard | Adding more mud | Stirring it every minute
test: Your sister wants to sort a big jar of mixed buttons fast. What is a good idea? | Use sieves with different holes | Use a magnet to pull out the plastic buttons | Dissolve them all in a bowl of water | Boil them in a big pan of water
test: Why does the salad spinner have holes in the inner basket but not the outer bowl? | Water escapes the basket and is caught in the bowl | Holes make the basket spin much faster | The bowl needs no holes because it is made of plastic | The holes let air dry the leaves
test: Why don't the salt bits rise with the steam from salty water? | Salt does not evaporate at boiling-water temperatures | Salt is too scared of heat | The salt sticks to the inside of the lid and stays there until it cools | Salt turns into steam first
test: "colander, sieve, tea bag, coffee filter, what's the same about 'em" Best answer? | They are all filters that let small bits through | They all use magnets | They all heat things up | They all dissolve whatever is put in them, so it can pass through
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the recovery of salt from a solution of salt in water? | The water is permitted to evaporate, the salt remaining as crystals | The solution is passed through filter paper, which retains the salt | A magnet is applied to the solution to extract the salt | The solution is left to stand until the salt floats
exam: Identify the method most appropriate to the separation of steel paper clips from sand. | The application of a magnet | Dissolution in water | Distillation | Chromatography
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning water that has been filtered through paper. | It may yet contain germs and dissolved substances and is not thereby rendered safe | It is invariably safe for consumption, all impurities having been retained by the paper | It contains no substance whatsoever save water | It is rendered sterile by the paper
exam: It is given that the steam from boiling salt water is condensed upon a cold surface. Ascertain the nature of the liquid obtained. | Pure water, devoid of salt | Water of greater salinity than the original | Salt in a liquid, molten state | A mixture identical to the original
